# Worktree hygiene — the tested tree is the shipped tree

Concurrent lanes each work in their own `git worktree` (`06-branching.mdc`).
A worktree left open between edits — by an agent across tool calls, by an
editor with the file loaded — has been observed to **revert edited files
to an earlier buffer** with no diff shown to whoever made the edit. This
rule exists because that has now happened in two independent lanes, and
the cause is environmental rather than anything either lane did (a third instance followed in the same lane, below).

## The observed failure

- **`wt-schain-r` (2026-09-16):** an uncommitted revert of a rename landed
  in the working tree after the edit had been made by tool; one of the
  reverted files would not have compiled.
- **`wt-stx` (2026-09-19):** the working copy of a plan doc drifted back to
  its *pre-verification* text — cite corrections that had been checked at
  source, committed, and pushed were undone in the working tree while the
  committed version stayed correct.
- **`wt-stx-inc` (2026-09-20):** the same lane, eight hours after a commit
  had been pushed and verified from a fresh clone: `git status` before a
  `dev` merge showed the plan doc modified, and the diff was the deletion
  of the decision-log row that commit had added. Caught by constraint 1
  (a file that was not edited showed as modified); restored from `HEAD`.
- **`wt-scurve` (2026-09-21) — the dangerous case, observed.** Two files
  drifted within twenty minutes of a commit: an archived plan's six
  repointed links reverted in the working copy (caught by the links gate,
  restored from `HEAD`); and two links in `IMPLEMENTATION_INDEX.md` reverted
  *and were then committed*, because the next commit staged that file whole
  for an unrelated edit — the stale content passed review as "the index
  row" and reached the remote. Explicit paths were used; the file was
  legitimately part of the commit. So constraint 1 is necessary and not
  sufficient: **before staging a file you edited, diff it against `HEAD`
  and read every hunk you did not make.** Fixed by a follow-up commit; the
  pushed tip was wrong for eight minutes.
- **`wt-salt` (2026-09-25) — the first instance caught by a mechanism
  rather than by vigilance.** Between a pre-flight commit and the ruling
  edit an hour later, `DRS_E1_SALT.md`'s working copy reverted to the buffer
  from *before* eleven line-number corrections had been applied and
  committed. Nobody noticed a modified file. What noticed was the edit
  itself: the ruling edit was an **anchored** script — replace exactly this
  text with exactly that — and it refused to run because the text it was
  written against was not there (`:4354` was `:4353` again). Nothing was
  written; the file was restored from `HEAD`; the edit was re-run against
  the restored text and applied cleanly. See "The edit form that notices".

Most of these were caught by a human or an agent reading the diff. That does not
scale, and it is not the dangerous case. **The dangerous case is a drift
followed by `git add -A` or `git commit -a`:** the stale content is
committed, and the tree that was tested and the tree that ships diverge
with nothing to notice. The drift specifically reverts *verified* edits to
what was wrong before someone checked — so what would be committed is
exactly the content the verification existed to remove.

## Two constraints, both mechanical

1. **Stage explicit paths. Never `git add -A`, `git add .`, `git add -u`,
   or `git commit -a` in a worktree that has been left open between edits.**
   Every commit names the files it carries. If a file you did not edit shows
   as modified, that is the signal — read the diff before touching it, and
   restore it from `HEAD` if it is a drift (`git checkout -- <path>`), not a
   change you made.

2. **Verify from a fresh checkout of the pushed SHA, not from the worktree.**
   Before reporting a PR head as green — and before resolving review
   findings against it — run the gates and tests in a checkout that
   contains only what was pushed:

   ```bash
   git clone -q --no-checkout --shared <repo> /tmp/verify-<pr>
   cd /tmp/verify-<pr> && git fetch -q origin <branch> && git checkout -q <sha>
   git submodule update --init --recursive -q
   # run the same gates CI runs, then delete the clone
   ```

   The worktree's gates are a fast local loop; they are not the
   verification. A clone of the pushed commit makes the tested artifact and
   the shipped artifact **the same object**, and it is the only mitigation
   here that does not depend on someone noticing.

## The edit form that notices

The fourteenth instance is worth more than the count, because the
detection did not come from a check designed to detect drift. It came from
an edit that **could not apply to text it was not written against**.

An anchored edit — `StrReplace` with enough context to be unique, a script
that asserts its `old` occurs exactly once before writing — is
self-verifying: it carries a claim about the file's current content, and a
drifted file falsifies the claim, so the edit fails loudly and writes
nothing. A **line-number** edit (`sed -i '4354s/…/…/'`) or a **wholesale
write** (`Write` of a whole file, a heredoc over it) carries no such claim:
it succeeds against whatever is there, and against a drifted file it
succeeds *silently* — which is exactly how eleven reverted line numbers
would have travelled into a commit under a tool that wrote the file whole.

So the durable mitigation is not another check. It is the form of the edit:

- **Treat the working copy as untrusted.** Not because it is usually wrong,
  but because when it is wrong nothing tells you.
- **Anchor every edit to the text it expects.** Prefer `StrReplace` and
  assert-count-then-replace scripts over line-addressed or whole-file
  writes for any file that has been sitting in a worktree between edits.
  When a wholesale write is unavoidable (a new file, a regeneration), diff
  the result against `HEAD` and read every hunk you did not intend.
- **An anchored edit that fails to match is a finding, not a nuisance.**
  Do not "fix" it by loosening the anchor or by re-reading the file and
  re-targeting the edit against what is there now. Run `git diff` on the
  file first: if the current text differs from `HEAD` in lines you did not
  touch this session, that is the drift, and `git checkout -- <path>` comes
  before the edit does.

Constraints 1 and 2 stand; this is the mechanism that makes constraint 1's
"read every hunk you did not make" happen by construction rather than by
attention.

## What this rule does not change

CI runs on the pushed SHA already, so a drift that reaches a push will
eventually fail there when it breaks a gate. This rule closes the two gaps
CI does not: a drift that passes gates while being *wrong* (a reverted
doc correction, a comment restored to a false claim), and the window
between "I ran the tests" and "CI is green" during which findings get
resolved against a tree that was never built.

## Related rules

- **`06-branching.mdc`** — worktrees, short-lived branches, each push
  separately authorized. This rule governs what is *in* the push.
- **`90-commits.mdc`** — scope per commit. Explicit staging is how a
  commit's scope is enforced, not just described.
- **`91-documentation-after-plans.mdc`** — the descriptive surface is
  checked by sweeps, not gates; a drift that restores a stale claim is the
  failure that rule's sweeps exist to catch, arriving through a new door.
